<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Apamea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">188 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-seleucid-war">Roman–Seleucid War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Apamea was the peace settlement of 188 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> after his defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a>. It stripped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> of their Anatolian territories beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, imposed 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> in war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and forbade them from keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and a substantial fleet. Accounts of the Seleucid Empire treat it as a decisive blow to Seleucid power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea.jpg" alt="Treaty of Apamea" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kryston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Apamea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
